### What version of gRPC are you using?
v1.57.0

### What version of Go are you using (`go version`)?
go version go1.21.1 windows/amd64

### What operating system (Linux, Windows, …) and version?
Windows

### What did you do?

I want to add resiliency to a system using gRPC my goal is to have the connection being able to restore itself (if it is not available at the moment, retry x times) and the RPC calls being able to retry on failures as well as not hanging on server-side (a timeout for long operations), for that I have defined a serviceConfig to which I provide to the withDefaultServiceConfig on the client side, it is defined as follows:

With this dialOpts, I configure the call to SayHello RPC
```go
dialOpts := []grpc.DialOption{grpc.WithTransportCredentials(insecure.NewCredentials()),
		grpc.WithDefaultServiceConfig(`
		{
			"methodConfig": [
				{
					"name": [
						{"service": "helloworld.Greeter", "method": "SayHello"}
					],
					"timeout": "2s",
					"retryPolicy": {
						"maxAttempts": 5,
						"initialBackoff": "0.1s",
						"maxBackoff": "1s",
						"backoffMultiplier": 2,
						"retryableStatusCodes": [
							"UNAVAILABLE", "DEADLINE_EXCEEDED"
						]
					}
				},
				{
					"name": [
						{"service": "", "method": ""}
					],
					"timeout": "10s",
					"retryPolicy": {
						"maxAttempts": 5,
						"initialBackoff": "0.1s",
						"maxBackoff": "1s",
						"backoffMultiplier": 2,
						"retryableStatusCodes": [
							"UNAVAILABLE", "DEADLINE_EXCEEDED"
						]
					}
				}
			]
		}`),
		grpc.WithContextDialer(func(ctx context.Context, addr string) (net.Conn, error) {
			print("dialing...")
			time.Sleep(5 * time.Second)
			return (&net.Dialer{}).DialContext(ctx, "tcp", addr)
		})}
```

As my understanding, the SayHello call will have a timeout of 2 seconds on the client side, but this code will always fails with a `DeadlineExceeded` after dialing one time for 2 seconds, and it will not retry.
Is it possible to set different timeouts for connection and RPC calls? Should I always consider this connection as part of the timeout I am setting, and then I am not able to define how log I will allow the server to perform an action? 

If I completely remove the timeout, using this service config:
```json
{
	"methodConfig": [
		{
			"name": [{"service": ""}],
			"waitForReady": true,
			"retryPolicy": {
				"MaxAttempts": 5,
				"InitialBackoff": "0.1s",
				"MaxBackoff": "10s",
				"BackoffMultiplier": 1.0,
				"RetryableStatusCodes": ["UNAVAILABLE", "RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED"]
			}
		}
	]
}
```
When I connect, using the same contextDialer I defined before, I see that the connection attempts will *never* stop. Is this the expected behavior? If I want to set only retry for my RPC calls I necessarily need to provide a timeout, otherwise my connections will hang forever?
I want to have a long-living bi-direction stream as well, if I set the timeout, will I not be able to have that? 

### What did you expect to see?
Connection retrying, and not hanging forever

### What did you see instead?
Connection hang, same timeout for everything
